  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Weathers gave them one earned run over six-plus innings. The lineup delivered one run total. That math doesn't work.",
    "what_this_means": "Ten strikeouts. Three runners left in scoring position. Not a single conversion. The offense showed up without actually showing up. Six hits that didn't matter, two hours of baseball that felt like survival when it should have felt like a chance. Weathers held. The bullpen couldn't hold what came after. Fernando Cruz and Tim Hill let the game slip in the eighth, four runs scoring when the score was still close enough to believe. This is what happens when good pitching meets no execution: you lose 1-5 at home and drop a four-game streak like it was never there. The problem isn't that they played bad baseball. It's that they played two different games at the same time. One team that pitched, one that didn't hit. Good pitching without offense is just a slower loss.",
    "what_to_watch": "Rhett Lowder brings a 4.60 ERA into tonight's matchup, and Cam Schlittler comes off a run where he's been sharp. 2.83 over his last four starts. This is the kind of game where the pitching advantage matters most.",
